## These are all common industry practices and do not, by themselves, reveal sensitive information.

Phase 1: Environment & Artifacts Initialization
[ ] Authenticate with the designated model artifact repository using secure developer credentials to acquire base model weights and tokenizer configurations.
[ ] Configure the local containerized runtime environment with hardware accelerator runtime extensions to enable direct compute passthrough.
[ ] Verify local driver availability, compute device memory headroom, and thermal thresholds to support target parameter sizing.

Phase 2: Inference Engine & API Pipeline Setup
[ ] Deploy the chosen high-throughput inference engine container image configured with optimized memory management and dynamic batching parameters.
[ ] Initialize the asynchronous application server backend template incorporating streaming response handlers.
[ ] Establish connection parameters and networking routes between the client application and the local inference engine REST endpoint.

Phase 3: Schema Enforcement & Security Hardening
[ ] Define strict structural typing models to validate incoming client payloads and enforce deterministic, structured outputs from the model.
[ ] Implement a pre-inference sanitization layer to strip disallowed control characters, scrub inputs, and mitigate injection vectors.
[ ] Enforce system instruction structural isolation using explicit token boundary delimiters to prevent instruction leakage or override.

Phase 4: Validation & Benchmarking
[ ] Execute benchmark utilities to measure time-to-first-token (TTFT) and sustained token generation throughput under concurrent load.
[ ] Run automated integration tests validating asynchronous error handling, chunk streaming delivery, and connection recovery.
[ ] Verify logging and telemetry output across specialized test vectors to confirm deterministic execution before integration.
